#include <cstdio>
#include <cstddef>

struct Tick {
    char   side;    // buy/sell flag
    double price;
    int    qty;
};

int main() {
    std::printf("sizeof=%zu alignof=%zu\n", sizeof(Tick), alignof(Tick));
    std::printf("side@%zu price@%zu qty@%zu\n",
        offsetof(Tick, side), offsetof(Tick, price), offsetof(Tick, qty));
}

#include <cstdio>

struct Base {
    virtual ~Base() = default;              // slots 0 and 1 in the vtable
    virtual double price(double s) const;   // slot 2
    double vol = 0.2;
};

struct Derived : Base {
    double spread = 0.01;
};

int main() {
    std::printf("Base=%zu Derived=%zu\n", sizeof(Base), sizeof(Derived));
}

#include <cstdio>

struct Slab {
    alignas(64) char bytes[64];   // exactly one cache line
};
static_assert(sizeof(Slab) == 64 && alignof(Slab) == 64);

int main() {
    std::printf("%zu\n", alignof(double));   // 8 on x86-64
}

guidedExercise.prompt:
Lay out this struct by hand on x86-64, then shrink it:

```cpp
struct Fill {
    char  flag;      // 'B' or 'S'
    double px;       // price
    int   qty;
    short venue;     // exchange id
};
```

Give every offset, the total sizeof, and then reorder members to minimise size and compute the new sizeof. Finally: with 10,000,000 fills in a vector, how many megabytes does the reordering save (1 MB = 10^6 bytes)?

independentExercise.prompt:
A risk system has:

```cpp
struct Instrument {
    virtual ~Instrument() = default;
    virtual double price() const;
    double mid;
};
struct Swap : Instrument {
    double spread;
    double notional;
};
```

(a) Give sizeof(Instrument) and sizeof(Swap) on x86-64 with member offsets. (b) How many vptrs does a Swap carry and why? (c) A vector holds 2,000,000 Instrument pointers pointing at mixed instruments - what does the vptr let each call site do, and what two operations does each `instr->price()` perform before entering the function body?

